## ABSTRACT
Selective hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to high value-added chemicals, not only gives an effective way to reduce the concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ ,  but  also  provides  the  precursors  to  advance  the  industrial  manufacturing  of  chemicals  and  fuels.  With  the  well-defined reticular  frameworks  and  flexible  modifiability,  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  can  be  the  ideal  platform  to  construct  the enabled catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation, because they have shown the great potential for the enhancement of catalytic activity, the precise control of selectivity, and the excellent stability. In this review, we systematically summarize the recent advances in MOFs based catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation towards diverse products. Firstly, synthesis strategies for different kinds of MOFs based catalysts are described. Secondly, selective hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ towards CO and methane is discussed over various metal nanoparticles/MOFs composites. Thirdly, heterogenization and isolation of molecular catalysts by MOFs are elaborated for producing formic acid. Fourthly, selective hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ toward methanol is discussed in terms of interface structures of Cu,  Zn,  and  metal  nodes  of  MOFs,  the  synergy  between  auxiliary  sites  and  noble  metal,  and  tandem  catalytic  systems  of molecular catalysts and Lewis acid sites. Subsequently, the integration of multiple metal sites, promoters, and cocatalysts into MOFs is described for the selective hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to ${\mathsf C}_{2+}$ products. After those, the key issue about the stability of MOFs based catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation reaction is discussed. Finally, the summary and perspective about MOFs based catalysts for selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation and mechanism research are proposed.
## KEYWORDS
selective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation,  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs),  noble  metal,  nonnoble  metal,  diverse  products, mechanism
## Introduction
The  excessive  emission  of  the  greenhouse  gas,  especially $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ which is linked to the consumption of fossil fuel, rapidly raises the average global temperature. According to the statistics, the average concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in atmosphere has almost increased by $48\%$ to $416~\mathrm{ppm}$ in 2020 since the start of Industrial Revolution [1–4]. For such a fast ascent, the global temperature rises less than $2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ this century and has been emphasized in Paris Agreement [5–7]. Besides the reduction of continuous emission, the removal of the existing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from  the  atmosphere  cannot  be  ignored  either. Compared to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and storage (CCS), $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and utilization  (CCU),  which  involves $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion,  can  exhibit great  promise  to  promote  industrial  development  and  even  the carbon cycle [8–12]. Among the current chemical technologies for CCU, selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has gradually aroused great interest  because  hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ is  green  and  sustainable  energy, which  can  be  continuously  produced  from  water $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ by photocatalysis  or  electrolysis  powered  by  solar,  wind,  and  so on [13–16].
The  research  on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  has  a  long  history,  and even  in  1897,  chemist  Paul  Sabatier  has  discovered  the  the hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  water  and  methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ ,  known  as Sabatier reaction [17]. Since 1980s, the hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has been  widely  studied,  and  different  products  like  CO,  methane, formic acid $\mathrm{(HCO_{2}H)}$ , methanol (MeOH), and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products can be obtained. To date, many kinds of catalysts have been reported for  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but  fewer  of  them  can  be  applied  in industrialization owing to some obstacles. First, it’s a challenge to activate  the  thermodynamically  stable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ because  it  has  the linear geometry and the bond enthalpy of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ bonds is as high as $+805\ \mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , meaning that high energy should be input to break the  bonds  [18–21].  As  for  traditional  supported  catalysts,  high temperature  is  often  required  for  achieving  a  satisfactory conversion rate of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Under such a harsh reaction condition, carbon emissions will increase, while the fast sintering of the active species cannot be avoided, causing the great difficulty for the longterm test [14, 22]. Therefore, it is urgently required for designing the enabled catalysts to realize hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under mild condition.  Second, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  often  involves  different reaction paths to get various products. For example, by a reverse water–gas  shift  (RWGS)  reaction,  CO  is  the  main  product, followed  by  further  hydrogenation  or  in  series  with  the
Fischer–Tropsch (FT) reaction to produce methane, methanol, or $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ alkanes and olefins [23–25]. Formic acid and methanol can be obtained via a formate pathway, and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ alcohols can be obtained via $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C}$ coupling with formyl (CHO) species, etc. [26–30]. Over practical  catalysts,  various  paths  will  coexist,  thus  generally reducing  the  selectivity  of  target  product  and  making  product separation difficult.
To solve these problems, it is urgent to rationally construct the enabled  catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation.  Different  from traditional  heterogeneous  supported  catalysts,  metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a class of crystalline materials, which have periodic network structures assembled by metal clusters or atoms and  organic  ligands,  can  act  as  the  ideal  platform  to  construct novel catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation under mild condition due to  the  following  possible  reasons  [31–37].  First,  the  rich  porous structure and high surface area of MOFs can effectively improve the dispersion of catalytic sites and the adsorption and activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ so that they can always catalyze $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation even at low temperatures [14, 38, 39]. Second, the metal nodes of MOFs can provide lots of Lewis acid or Brønsted acid sites, which can contribute to activating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or stabilizing some key intermediates [40, 41]. Third, different isolated catalytic sites can be incorporated into MOFs with strong metal–support interactions (SMSIs), and the coordination environment can be adjusted on purpose easily to  control  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  because  MOFs  can  be  easily tailored  and  modified  [42–45].  Finally,  using  the  pore confinement effect can improve the distribution of catalytic sites and enhance their stability during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation [46–48].
With the rapid development of MOFs in the past two decades, some  MOFs  have  displayed  the  good  thermal  and  chemical stability  during  the  catalytic  process  [49–51].  Since  2015,  MOFs have  been  gradually  applied  for  catalytic  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [52–54]. Although some progresses have been made, the research on  the  design  of  catalytic  sites,  catalytic  performance,  and mechanism  understanding  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  is  in  the ascending stage of experience accumulation. Also, there are only few reviews involving the discussion on selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over MOFs based catalysts [21, 55–57]. Thus, it is necessary to summarize the recent progress on the emerging MOFs based catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation and give some novel insights for achieving high catalytic efficiency.
In  this  review,  we  have  systematically  summarized  the construction of MOFs based catalysts for selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ towards  corresponding  products,  like  CO,  methane, formic  acid,  methanol,  and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products,  as  well  as  the structure–performance relationships. Firstly, the synthesis methods  for  different  MOFs  based  catalysts  are  summarized. Secondly, the size effect, metal dispersion, and microstructure of metal  nanoparticles/MOFs  composites  for  producing  CO  and methane are discussed. Thirdly, the heterogenization and isolation of  molecular  catalysts  by  MOFs  are  elaborated  for  producing formic  acid.  Fourthly,  interface  structures  of $\mathrm{{Cu},Z n,}$ and  metal nodes  of  MOFs,  the  synergy  between  auxiliary  sites  and  noble metal,  and  tandem  catalytic  systems  of  molecular  catalysts  and Lewis acid sites are illustrated for selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ toward methanol. Subsequently, the integration of multiple metal sites,  promoters,  and  cocatalysts  into  MOFs  is  described  for  the selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products. After those, the key  issue  about  stability  of  MOFs  based  catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation is discussed. Finally, the summary and the future outlook  about  MOFs  based  catalysts  for  selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation and mechanism research are proposed.
## 2    Synthesis  of  MOFs  based  catalysts  for $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation
The synthesis strategies of MOFs have been well developed in the last two decades, and various mature synthesis methods have been widely  recognized  and  applied,  such  as  solvothermal  synthesis, microwave-assisted  synthesis,  electrochemical  synthesis,  and mechanochemical synthesis, etc. As a kind of crystalline network frameworks,  the  abundant  periodic  pore  structures  and  the modification  of  metal  nodes  or  organic  linkers  of  MOFs  enable the  facile  construction  of  catalytic  sites  within  MOFs  at  the molecular level. In this section, we will give a brief summary of constructing MOFs based catalysts for selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
The modification of metal nodes in MOFs is a valuable method to construct catalytic sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation. Post-synthetic modification  (PSM)  of  metal-oxo  clusters  in  secondary  building units  (SBUs)  of  MOFs  can  introduce  active  metal  sites  by deprotonating $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ or $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH}$ to  bind  other  metal  centers [27, 58–60]. For example, in the zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr})$ trimesate MOF808, treating $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(\upmu_{3}-O)_{4}(\upmu_{3}-O H)_{4}}$ (carboxylate) $\mid_{12}$ SBUs with $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ can  deprotonate $\mathrm{Zr_{3}(\upmu_{3}-O H)}$ groups  to  generate $\mathrm{Zr_{3}(\upmu_{4}-O)Z n E t}$ moieties  and  then  the  neighboring $Z\mathrm{n-Zr}$ sites  can  be  obtained [58]. ${\mathrm{LiCH}}_{2}{\mathrm{SiMe}}_{3}$ was selected to deprotonate the $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ and $\upmu_{2}-$ OH  of $\mathrm{Zr_{12}O_{8}(\upmu_{3}-O H)_{8}(\upmu_{2}-O H)_{6}}$ (carboxylate) $^{18}$ SBUs  of $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ MOFs  to  form  the  exposed $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O^{-}}}$ and $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O^{-}}$ ,  followed  by metalation  with $\mathrm{[Cu^{\mathrm{I}}(C H_{3}C N)_{4}](B F)_{4}}$ for  anchoring  the  multiple ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ cluster [27].
In addition, the organic linkers in MOFs can be functionalized with transition metal ions by PSM. Using this method, the partial replacement of organic linker 1,1’-biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\dag}}$ -dicarboxylic acid (bpdc)  in $\mathrm{zr}$ MOF  UiO-67  by $^{2,2^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine-5,5’-dicarboxylic acid  (bpydc)  could  provide  the  sites  for  coordinating  with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ Combined with the $Z\mathrm{n}$ anchored on metal nodes, the bimetallic CuZn@UiO-bpy catalysts were synthesized successfully via in situ reduction  [61].  Furthermore,  the  post-synthetic  modification  of the organic linkers can realize the heterogenization of molecular catalysts. Amino groups of chromium $\mathrm{(Cr)}$ terephthalate MIL-101- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ reacting with salicylaldehyde, and 2- diphenylphosphinobenzaldehyde  (DPPBde)  via  the  Schiff  base reaction  could  create  a  five-membered  ring  to  coordinate  with ${\mathrm{RuCl}}_{3}$ [62].
Besides,  porous  MOFs  are  the  ideal  platfrom  for  supporting metal nanoparticles owing to the high surface areas, controllable structure,  and  easy  modifiability.  Typically,  impregnation reduction  method  was  widely  used  to  gain  supported  catalysts. First, the metal precursors and MOFs are mixed through vaporphase  deposition  or  liquid-phase  impregnation  methods  (IMs). Then,  the  nanoparticles  was  formed  as  the  participate  via reduction  using  different  agents  such  as $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}},}$ citric  acid, ammonia  borane,  hydrazine,  or  hydrogen.  For  example,  using hydrogen  as  the  reducing  agent,  palladium  (Pd)  nanoparticles have  been  successfully  anchored  on $\mathrm{HZSM-}5@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ toward  CO  [63].  Besides,  gold  (Au) nanoparticles on the UiO-67 could be easily prepared by reducing $\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}}$ with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ [64].  In  addition,  the  adsorption  of  assynthesized metal nanoparticles by MOFs is another strategy for synthesis of metal nanoparticles/MOFs composites.
To  fully  utilize  the  well-defined  pores  of  MOFs,  various methods  have  been  developed  to  incorporate  catalytic  sites  into MOFs,  such  as  the  one-pot  synthesis,  double-solvent  method (DSM)  and  the  aperture-opening  strategy.  As  for  one-pot synthesis, metal nanoparticles are first prepared and then MOFs are  formed  around  the  functional  nanoparticles.  Yaghi  et  al. utilized one-pot method to coat zirconium terephthalic UiO-66 on the  surface  of  copper  nanocrystals  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to methanol [65]. In addition, the core–shell nanoparticles $\operatorname{Au}\ @\operatorname{Pd}$ were  synthesized  firstly  and  then  MOF-74  nanoshuttles  could grow  around  them  to  inhibit  the  migration  and  sintering  of nanoparticles [66]. As for DSM, MOFs are generally dispersed in hydrophobic solvent like n-hexane, and then metal precursors in aqueous  solution  entered  the  more  hydrophilic  pores  of  MOFs with constant vigorous stirring or ultrasound. Using this method, the  nickel  ion $(\mathrm{Ni^{2+}})$ precursors  entered  the  pores  of  chromium terephthalate MIL-101, and further were reduced by hydrogen for $24\mathrm{h}$ at $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to get the Ni nanoparticles confined in the pores of MIL-101 [67]. In 2014, Tsung et al. reported the aperture-opening strategy  which  takes  advantage  of  ligand  exchange  reactions  to open  part  of  the  framework  of  the  resynthesized  ZIF-8  crystals and  allow  large  guest  molecules  to  diffuse  into  the  MOF  pores [68].  Using  this  approach,  the  linker  dissociation  of  MOFs happened in a protic solvent, like methanol to form an apertureopened intermediate. Thus, the molecular catalysts larger than the pore  size  of  MOFs  host  can  enter  the  pores  by  the  opened aperture, followed by the dynamic linker association. Next, in the catalytic  process  taken  place  in  an  aprotic  solvent,  like dimethylformamide  (DMF),  the  linker  dissociation  will  be suppressed and the leaching of molecular guests into the solution can  be  prevented.  Similarly,  the  ruthenium $\mathrm{(Ru)}$ complex $(\mathrm{^{tBu}P N P)R u(C O)\mathrm{-}]}$ HCl $\mathrm{{'}t B u p{N P}}=2{,}6$ -bis((di-tert-butyl-phosphino) methyl)pyridine)  has  been  successfully  encapsulated  by  UiO-66 framework in protic solvent methanol [69].
In  brief,  various  synthesis  strategies  have  been  developed  for constructing  the  enabled  MOFs  based  catalysts  for  selective hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .  The  modification  of  metal  nodes  and organic  linkers  brings  more  ideas  for  the  preparation  of  MOFs based catalysts. On the other hand, benefiting from the abundant pores and high surface area of MOFs, the metal nanoparticles and molecular catalysts were easily combined with MOFs. However, it is  still  highly  required  for  improving  the  uniformity  of  catalytic sites,  and  avoiding  their  exfoliation  and  agglomeration  via  new convenient methods.
## 3    Reverse water–gas shift to CO
Reverse  water–gas  shift  reaction  is  one  of  the  most  potential pathways for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion and the produced CO can act as the important  component  of  syngas  for  producing  methanol,  light olefins, and liquid fuels in FT process, or act as the important raw material for DMF [23, 25, 29, 70–72]. Generally, the activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ needs  high  energy  input  and  the  formation  of  CO  is thermodynamically  endothermic  according  to  Eq.  (1)  [1, 23].
Thus, high reaction temperature is always applied for producing CO
$$
\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}{\rightleftharpoons}\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}~\Delta H_{\mathrm{298K}}=+41.2\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}
$$
Traditional supported platinum $\left(\mathrm{Pt}\right)$ catalysts have been widely studied with the high activity and selectivity toward CO due to the cooperation  of $\mathrm{Pt}$ and  reducible  oxide  supports  [73–75],  while other metal catalysts like Pd [76], Au [77], rhodium (Rh) [78], and iron (Fe) have also been developed [79]. However, at high reaction temperature, the sintering of small metal sites on traditional oxide supports cannot be avoided, which could severely affect the CO yield  and  stability  of  catalysts  [74].  Besides,  achieving  high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate and high CO selectivity, especially the competition between  CO  desorption  and  further  hydrogenation  of  adsorbed CO to methane, is still a big challenge [22, 80, 81].
To  address  above  issues,  MOFs  can  be  used  to  support  and stabilize small sized metal sites, which can weaken CO adsorption to  obtain  high  yield  and  avoid  the  sintering  of  metal  sites  with high stability (Table 1). For example, Olsbye et al. synthesized the Zr  MOF  UiO-67  characteristic  with  the  mixing  ligands  of  bpdc and bpydc, and Pt nanoparticles were well anchored on UiO-67 (UiO-67-Pt)  with  average  size  of $2.6\ \mathrm{\nm}$ due  to  the  strong coordination  between $\mathrm{Pt}$ and  bipyridine  sites.  As  shown  in Fig. 1(a),  when  used  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  at $240~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient pressure $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}6$ ), UiO-67-Pt showed $7.5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate, higher than $\sim6.5\%$ for $\mathrm{Pt/SiO}_{2}$ characteristic with $4~\mathrm{nm}~\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles,  while  both  showed  the  similar  CO selectivity  above $97\%$ .  Besides,  UiO-67-Pt  showed  the  excellent stability  over $52\mathrm{~h~}$ reaction  [82].  In  addition,  UiO-67  supported Au  nanoparticles $(\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67)$ )  were  prepared  by  using $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}},}$ and  oleylamine  as  reductants,  respectively,  and corresponding size of Au nanoparticles on UiO-67 is 9.9, 13.9, and $12\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1(b)). When used as catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation at $408~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $2\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=1{:}5)$ ), $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ exhibited the highest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $38\%$ in respect with $34.5\%$ for $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{UiO-}67\mathrm{-NaBH_{4}},$ and $15\%$ for Au@UiO-67-oleylamine, while all  of  them  showed  the  similar  CO  selectivity  of $96.5\%$ . Furthermore,  the  catalytic  activity  is  well  maintained  over $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for $50\mathrm{h}$ on steam at such high temperature [64]. Altogether,  the  porous  nature  and  functional  ligands  of  MOFs enhance  their  ability  to  anchor  and  stabilize  the  well  dispersed metal nanoparticles with small sizes, which can show the excellent catalytic performance for producing CO.
In  addition  to  the  size  effect  of  metal  sites,  the  composite  of MOFs with zeolite or molecular sieve can be applied to improve the yield of CO catalyzed by metal sites. For example, Xu et al. synthesized  the  core–shell  support  by  coating  zirconium  2- aminoterephthalate UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ membrane on zeolite HZSM-5, and then, Pd nanoparticles were well dispersed on the membrane surface  to  form  the  catalyst, $\mathrm{HZSM–5@UiO–66–NH_{2}/P d}$ (Fig. 2(a)). Due to the strong coordination between Pd and the amino groups of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ , Pd nanoparticles were highly dispersed on HZSM- ${\cdot}5@\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with average size of $2.3~\mathrm{nm}$ , similar with Pd  on  single ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ .  When  used  as  catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  at $320~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $2\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3\mathrm{)}$ ,  HZSM$5@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}$ showed the slightly lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $17.1\%$ than $25.4\%$ for $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd},$ but it exhibited much higher  CO  selectivity  of $92.2\%$ than $30.9\%$ over $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Furthermore, $\mathrm{HZSM-}5@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}$ showed  the  good stability over $40\mathrm{{h}}$ reaction. The difference in CO selectivity can be ascribed to the case that HZSM-5 effectively reduced the binding energy of CO on Pd sites, and inhibited its further hydrogenation to  methane,  thus  resulting  in  the  higher  selectivity  of  CO  on $\mathrm{HZSM-}5@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{Pd}$ .  Besides,  the  strong  coordination between  Pd  and  amino  groups  of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\-}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ effectively prevented  the  ultrasmall  Pd  nanoparticles  from  agglomeration [63].  In  another  work,  the  nano-bricks  silicoaluminophosphate (SAPO)  molecular  sieves  were  coated  by  zirconium  terephthalic UiO-66  membrane  with  average  thickness  of $13.6\ \mathrm{\nm}$ ,  and $2.05\ \mathrm{~nm}$ Pt  nanoparticles  were  loaded  on  this  membrane $(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{SAPO}{-}34@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66)$ ,  a  little  smaller  than $2.44~\mathrm{nm}$ on  single UiO-66 (Pt/UiO-66) and $2.84\ \mathrm{nm}$ on single SAPO (Pt/SAPO-34). When used as catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 3 MPa $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\ =\ 1{:}3\right)$ ,  Pt/SAPO-34@UiO-66  exhibited  the  CO yield  of $24.3\%$ and  selectivity  of $89.2\%$ ,  higher  than  those  over
Pt/SAPO-34 and Pt/UiO-66. Thus, the combination of SAPO-43 and UiO-66 could promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation towards CO [83].
In  short,  the  porous  nature  and  functional  ligands  of  MOFs enhance  their  ability  to  strongly  immobilize  the  well-dispersed metal  nanoparticles  like $\mathrm{Pt,}$ Au,  and  Pd  with  small  sizes  and weaken  CO  adsorption.  These  can  efficiently  increase  the conversion  rate  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  CO  selectivity,  and  even  avoid  the sintering of metal sites. In addition to the size effect of metal sites, combining  MOFs  with  zeolites  or  molecular  sieves  as  the supports,  or  integrating  multiple  catalytic  sites  into  MOFs  can efficiently  facilitate  the  activation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  the  desorption  of CO. In the future, to further improve the yield of CO, anchoring the  metal  sites  with  high  valence  states  or  single  metal  sites  on MOFs would be the good choice due to their low adsorption for CO.
## 4 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ methanation
Methane, as the main component of natural gas, can be used as the precursor for chemical production or directly as the fuel [85]. The  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  methane,  also  called  Sabatier reaction, is an important process for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. To date, $\mathrm{Ru},$ Rh, $\mathrm{Ni},$ and $\mathrm{Co}$ supported on various oxide, like $\mathrm{CeO}_{2},\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{SiO}_{2},$ . and $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}.$ ,  have  been  applied  for  the  Sabatier  reaction  [86–89]. Generally,  hard  conditions,  especially  the  high  temperature,  are required  for  providing  high  energy  input  and  overcoming  the limit  in  activity,  due  to  the  thermodynamically  stability  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  the  eight-electron  transfer  process  for  methane  production [72, 88, 90, 91]. However, according to Eq. (2), $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  methane  is  an  exothermic  reaction  while  the formation  of  CO  by  RWGS  is  thermodynamically  endothermic (Eq.  (1))  [90].  High  reaction  temperature  will  inhibit  the formation  of  methane  and  lead  to  a  high  proportion  of  CO  in products
$$
\mathbf{CO}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}_{2}\rightleftharpoons C\mathrm{H}_{4}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}\ \Delta H_{\mathrm{298~K}}=165\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}
$$
To  date,  Ni  catalysts  have  exhibited  the  strong  adsorption  of CO  intermediate  during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  and  could  make further hydrogenation of CO towards methane [22, 23]. With the high surface area of MOFs, the highly dispersed Ni sites can be immobilized  by  MOFs,  thus  resulting  in  a  significant enhancement in activity even at low temperatures. For example, Ni nanoparticles with average size of $9\mathrm{nm}$ have been immobilized on the surface of zinc terephthalate MOF-5 (Ni/MOF-5) by the impregnation method. In the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation reaction, Ni/MOF5 showed a higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $47.2\%$ than $34\%$ over $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ at $280~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient pressure $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}=1{:}4)}$ ). Besides, Ni/MOF-5 showed almost no loss of activity over $80\mathrm{h}$ on steam. The  excellent  performance  is  mainly  ascribed  to  the  higher dispersion of $41.8\%$ for Ni nanoparticles over MOF-5 than $33.7\%$ for $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ over $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ as well as the strong metal–support interaction between Ni nanoparticles and MOF-5 [52]. In addition, the welldefined pores of MOFs can confine the Ni nanoparticles, and thus, increase the dispersion and stability. Zirconium terephthalate UiO66  was  used  to  encapsulate  Ni  nanoparticles $\mathrm{(Ni@UiO–66)}$ with average size of $2\mathrm{nm}$ . When used as catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation at $320~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=1{:}3\right)$ ),  Ni@UiO-66  showed  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $68.2\%$ , $100\%$ selectivity of methane, and turnover frequency (TOF) of $4.3\times10^{-2}\:s^{-1}$ , much higher than those of $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}@Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ and  moreover,  it  had  the  lowest activation  energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ of $68.9\ \mathrm{kJ\cdotmol^{-1}}$ .  In  addition,  the  catalytic performance is well maintained for $\boldsymbol{100}\mathrm{h}$ on steam over ${\mathrm{Ni@UiO-}}$ 66. The higher dispersion of small Ni nanoparticles in UiO-66 by the pore confinement was responsible for the outstanding activity enhancement.  And  with  the  confinement  by  the  pores,  the sintering of Ni nanoparticles could be avoided [92].
By the SMSIs between MOFs and $\mathrm{Ni},$ the crystal properties of Ni sites can be adjusted to further improve their catalytic activity. For  example,  Ni  nanoparticles  were  encapsulated  by  chromium terephthalate MIL-101 with the similar high dispersion of about $40\%$ and average size of about $3\mathrm{nm}$ via IM (Ni@MIL-101(IM)) or DSM (Ni@MIL-101(DSM)) method. When used for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation  at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and  1  bar $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\rightleftharpoons1:4)$ , $\mathrm{{Ni@MIL}}$ - 101(DSM)  showed  a  higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  rate  of $100\%$ and  TOF  of $1.63~\times~10^{-3}~{\bf{S}}^{-1};$ ,  and  lower  activation  energy  of $88.01\ \mathrm{kJ\cdotmol^{-1}}$ than $68.9\%$ , $1.12\times10^{-3}\:s^{-1}$ , and $146.43\ \mathrm{kJ\cdotmol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{{Ni@MIL-101(IM)}}$ .  Moreover,  Ni@MIL-101(DSM)  showed  no decrease  in  catalytic  activity  over $200~\mathrm{~h~}$ on  steam.  The characterization results showed that Ni@MIL-101(DSM) had the higher proportion of exposed Ni (111) facet to (200) facet of 1.80 than that of Ni@MIL-101(IM). Density functional theory (DFT) calculation  showed  that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was  adsorbed  and  dissociated  to $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ by  direct  C–O  bond  cleavage  while  hydrogen  was dissociated on Ni (Fig. 3). The $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ reacted with the H species to form $\mathrm{CHO}_{\mathrm{ads}},$ followed  by  further  hydrogenation  to $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}_{\mathrm{ads}},$ which  would  be  dissociated  to $\mathrm{CH}_{2\mathrm{ads}}.$ .  Finally,  methane  was formed by the continuous hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CH}_{2\mathrm{ads}}$ . There was a lower  energy  barrier  of $10.0\ \mathrm{kcal{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dissociation  to $\mathrm{CO}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ on Ni (111) than $20.3\mathrm{kcal{\cdot}m o l^{-1}}$ over Ni (200), meaning that Ni (111) could promote the dissociation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to improve the catalytic activity. Besides, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ temperature programmed reduction (TPR)  measurement  showed  that  Ni@MIL-101(DSM)  had  the reduction  peak  at $252^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ lower  than $267~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for  Ni@MIL101(IM). The Ni species of $\mathrm{Ni@MIL-101(DSM)}$ was more easily reduced  confirming  that  there  was  a  stronger  metal–support interaction  between  Ni  and  MIL-101,  which  was  the  main contributor to the excellent stability of Ni@MIL-101(DSM) [67].
In addition to Ni sites, other metal sites anchored on MOFs can also  catalyze $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  methane  at  a  relatively  low temperature  with  the  assistance  of  plasma.  The  plasma  is  an ionized  gas  which  consists  of  positive  and  negative  ions  or electrons. These high energetic charged particles can replace high temperature heating and input enough energy for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation. Di  et  al.  synthesized  a  mesoporous  UiO-66  as  the  support  to immobilize Ru nanoparticles by in situ reduction during reaction. When used for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation, with the assistance of a dielectric barrier discharge plasma system, the catalyst showed a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $41.3\%$ and methane selectivity of $94.6\%$ at $0.1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ without  extra  heating.  The  synergistic  effect  between plasma  and  high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption  capacity  of  UiO-66  efficiently improved  the  yield  of  methane  [94].  To  further  improve  the dispersion  of $\mathtt{R u}$ sites,  Ru  nanoparticles  with  average  size  of $1.66\mathrm{nm}$ were in situ encapsulated within the rich pores of UiO-66. When they were used as catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  rate  was  improved  up  to $72.2\%$ with  a  methane selectivity of $95.4\%$ at $0.1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ with the plasma assistance due to the higher dispersion of Ru sites [95]. In addition, the modulation of  the  microenvironment  in  MOFs  has  also  been  exploited  to participate in the plasma-assisted catalysis. UiO-66 was covered by $\mathrm{Co}$ -formic acid MOF followed by thermal reduction in hydrogen to  form  the  catalyst,  named $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF.  Besides,  after  being impregnated by $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2},$ UiO-66 was further reduced to form the  other  catalyst  named $\mathrm{Co/Zr\mathrm{-}M O F(I M P)}$ .  The  transmission electron  microscopy  (TEM)  image  and  elemental  mapping showed that $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ oxide nanoparticles with average size of $16\ \mathrm{nm}$ were  uniformly  dispersed  on ${\mathrm{Co/Zr–MOF}}$ while  those  with  the size  of $0.52\ \mathrm{nm}$ were  observed  on $\mathrm{Co/Zr\mathrm{-}M O F(I M P)}$ .  In $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation  reaction, ${\mathrm{Co/Zr–MOF}}$ showed  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate of $58.9\%$ and methane selectivity of $94.7\%$ , higher than $24.8\%$ and $9.8\%$ over $\mathrm{Co/Zr\mathrm{-}M O F(I M P)}$ at $0.1\ \mathrm{MPa}$ with  the  plasma assistance.  The  rich  pores  and  larger  surface  area  of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ oxide nanoparticles  formed  from  porous  Co-MOF  could  enhance  the adsorption  and  activation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  improve  the  catalytic activity.  And  X-ray  photoelectron  spectroscopy  (XPS)  spectra confirmed that there was a higher ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$ sites on the surface  of $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF  than $\mathrm{Co/Zr\mathrm{-}M O F(I M P),}$ which  was beneficial for methane production. The synergy between plasma assistance  and  modulation  of  microenvironment  was  the  main contributor  to  the  excellent  catalytic  performance  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation over $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF [96].
Ni sites by MOFs can be the effective way for the enhancement of catalytic performance. As for other metal sites, the synergy with plasma  system  can  efficiently  promote  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation without extra heating.
## 5 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formic acid
Formic  acid  can  be  widely  used  in  leather  production,  animal feeds additives, and silage preservation [97, 98]. Moreover, formic acid is considered as a suitable liquid hydrogen storage chemical [99]. Hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formic acid is an excellent way for energy  storage  and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilization.  Since  Yoshio  et  al.  firstly reported $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{4}$ $\mathrm{(PPh}_{3}$ =  triphenylphosphine)  and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{PPh}_{3})_{3}$ (Ir:  Iridium)  as  catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to formic  acid  [100],  molecular  catalysts,  especially  based  on Ru [101, 102], Ir [103], and Rh [104], have exhibited high activity and  excellent  selectivity  for  formic  acid  production.  But  the conversion  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  formic  acid  always  occurs  in  aqueous solution  with  a  negative  free  energy  change $(\Delta G=-4~\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ ) because the reaction in gas phase involves a positive free energy change $(\Delta G=33\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}, $ ) [105, 106]. Molecular catalysts are used in a homogeneous solution system, meaning that the separating and  recycling  of  catalysts  will  need  tedious  steps.  Besides,  the bimolecular  catalyst  deactivation  occurs  easily  in  solution  [107]. To address above, the heterogenization and isolation of molecular catalysts  by  MOFs  can  be  the  effective  way  for  avoiding  the bimolecular catalyst deactivation, thus achieving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formic acid with high activity, great selectivity, and good stability.
Tsung et al. reported that the rich pores of MOFs can be used as the chambers to encapsulate size-selective molecular catalysts by the  aperture-opening  strategy  based  on  dissociative  linker exchange.  UiO-66  was  applied  to  encapsulate  the  molecular catalyst $(\mathrm{^{tBu}P N P)R u(C O)–H C}$ l  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  formic acid as shown in Fig. 5(a). It is noted that $[\mathrm{Ru}]@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66$ exhibited a  high  turnover  number  (TON)  of  280,000,  slightly  lower  than about 330,000 for homogeneous molecular catalysts, even at very low temperature $27^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 15 bar $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}=1{:}4)}$ ) after $0.5\mathrm{h}$ with 1,8-diazabicyclo[5.4.0]undec-7-ene  (DBU)  as  the  base  in  DMF. Such  a  high  TON  of $[\mathrm{Ru}]@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ remained  almost  the  same during the successive five cycles while homogeneous $({}^{\mathrm{tBu}}\mathrm{PNP})\mathrm{Ru(CO)\mathrm{-}I}$ HCl showed a significant reduction in activity at the  second  cycle  because  of  the  bimolecular  deactivation  (Fig. $5(\mathrm{b})_{.}^{.}$ ).  The  coordination  environment  of  active  Ru  sites  was  still maintained  after  encapsulation,  and  thus,  showed  the  high catalytic efficiency. And it is worth noting that the size of the Ru based molecular catalyst is $13.293\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , larger than the aperture size of  UiO-66  (11.136  Å),  but  slightly  smaller  than  the  pore  size $(15.748\mathrm{\AA})$ ). Therefore, only one molecule could be encapsulated in one chamber and was prevented from leaching into solution with the pore confinement, which could keep the isolation of molecular catalysts, and make the recycle easier [69].
Compared to those of traditional supports, the ligands of MOFs are easier modified to integrate metal sites and electron donating groups  and  imitate  the  molecular  catalysts.  The  multidentate ligands in molecular catalysts that have electron donating groups such as pyridyl nitrogen, phosphine, and others play a critical role in the high catalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formic acid, like $({}^{\mathrm{tBu}}\mathrm{PNP})\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO}){\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{HCl}$ , $\mathrm{[IrI_{2}(A c O)}$ (bis-NHC)] $(\mathrm{NHC}\ =\ \mathrm{N}.$ heterocyclic  carbene)  [101, 108].  Because  they  can  improve  the electron  density  of  the  metal  sites  and  increase  the  hydricity  of formed metal-hydride intermediates, and thus, further accelerate the  transfer  of  hydride  species  to $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ resulting  in  a  faster insertion  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  metal-hydride  [14, 109].  By  a  Schiff  base reaction with amino groups, salicylaldehyde and DPPBde can be linked onto the ligands of $\mathrm{MIL-}101(\mathrm{Cr}){\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ ions were further  anchored  on  the  chelating  groups  to  form  the  catalysts $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sal}$ and $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}.$ -DPPB (Fig. $5(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formic acid at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $6\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}=1{:}4)}$ with triethylamine $(\mathrm{Et}_{3}\mathrm{N})$ as the base in  ethanol, $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –101\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{DPPB}$ showed  the  highest catalytic activity with a TON of 242 after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction, in respect with  218  over $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101-\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Sal}$ and  198  over $\mathrm{RuCl_{3}@M I L-101{\cdot}N H_{2}}$ (Fig. 5(d)). The reaction mechanism study showed that $\mathrm{Ru-H}$ was formed firstly, followed by hydride transfer to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for forming the formate intermediate, which was further protonated to get formic acid. Among them, the rate determining step, insertion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{Ru-H}$ was promoted by the bidentate chelating  groups  of  salicylaldehyde  and  DPPBde  as  the  electron donating groups. And the stronger electron donating ability of $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ sites in DPPBde made the highest electron density of Ru sites in $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-DPPB}$ ,  resulting  in  its  highest  catalytic activity in respect with $\mathrm{RuCl_{3}@M I L\ –101-N H_{2}}^{-}$ Sal and ${\mathrm{RuCl}}_{3}@{\mathrm{MIL}}.$ ${101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ .  XPS  spectra  further  showed  that  the  Ru  species  for $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –101\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{DPPB}$ had  the  lowest  binding  energy  of $463.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , in respect with $463.4~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{RuCl}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101-\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ al and $463.5\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{RuCl_{3}}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –101\ –\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ [62]. In addition, Mehlana et al. synthesized Mg and magnesium (Mn) based MOFs with $^{2,2^{\circ}}$ - bipyridine- $\cdot4,4^{\cdot}$ -dicarboxylate  as  the  ligands,  named  Mn:JMS-2 and $\mathrm{Mg}{:}\mathrm{JMS}{-}2$ ,  and  Pd  sites  were  anchored  by  the  electrondonating  pyridyl  nitrogen  groups.  When  used  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=1{:}4\right)$ in ethanol with KOH as the base over $24\mathrm{h},$ the TOF values were $409{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ for Pd@Mn:JMS-2 and $303~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Mg}{:}\mathrm{JMS-}2$ , both higher than $170\mathrm{{h}^{-1}}$ over Pd anchored on $^{2,2^{\circ}}$ -bipyridine- $^{4,4^{\dag}}$ -dicarboxylate as the homogeneous catalysts. This can be ascribed to the case that deactivation  could  happen  on  the  homogeneous  catalysts,  while the heterogenization of metal sites by coordination with electron donation groups of MOFs can enhance their stability [110].
In  addition  to  electron  donation  groups,  extra  functional groups can also be introduced by the modification of ligands of MOFs  for  improving  the  catalytic  efficiency  of  metal  sites.  For example,  Lin  et  al.  synthesized  zirconium  UiO-type  MOFs  with bpdc and bpydc as the ligands. The bipyridine sites were used to immobilize ${\mathrm{IrCl}}_{3}$ to get mbpy- $\mathrm{.IrCl}_{3}$ -UiO. Followed by treatment with $\mathrm{NaBHEt}_{3}$ and air oxidation, the chloride was removed and replaced  by  OH  or $\mathrm{OH}_{2}$ to  get  the  catalyst,  mbpy- $[\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{m}}]$ -UiO. Besides, mbpyOH- $[\mathrm{Ir^{\mathrm{III}}}]$ -UiO was synthesized by introducing extra OH substitution on the 6-position of ligands. When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  formic  acid  at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and  ambient  pressure $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}=1:1)}$ ) over $15\mathrm{h}$ , mbpyOH- $[\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{m}}]$ -UiO showed the highest TON of $^{6,149}$ , in respect with 417 over mbpy- $\left[\mathrm{Ir}^{\mathrm{III}}\right]$ -UiO and 23 over  mbpy- $\mathrm{.IrCl}_{3}$ -UiO,  as  shown  in Fig. 6(a).  The $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}$ kinetic isotope effects (KIEs) experiments and DFT calculation confirmed that proton-hydride transfer was the rate-determining step (Figs. $6(\mathrm{b})$ and $6(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ).  The  OH  and  the  pyridyl  nitrogen  acted  as  the electron  donating  groups  to  promote  the  hydride  transfer  from $\mathrm{Ir{-}H}$ to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .  And  the  OH  substitution  on  the  6-position  of ligands  could  act  as  the  extra  hydrogen-bonding  site  to  further synergize  with  electron  donating  groups  and  facilitate  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation.  Interestingly,  as  shown  in Fig. $6(\mathrm{d})$ ,  the  reaction was  performed  in  a  soxhlet-type  reflux-condensing  system  for constructing a dynamic multi-phasic interface of gas/liquid/solid to maximize the contact among gas, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , and catalysts [111].
In short, metal molecular catalysts can be encapsulated within the  pores  of  MOFs  by  the  aperture-opening  strategy  or  being directly grafted on the ligands of MOFs by PSM, which can realize the  heterogenization  and  isolation  of  molecular  catalysts  and overcome  the  bimolecular  deactivation  and  difficulty  in separation. In addition, integrating electron donating groups with metal  sites  into  MOFs  for  imitating  the  molecular  catalysts  can effectively promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formic acid because the electron  donating  groups  can  play  a  key  role  in  promoting  the insertion  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into  M-H  intermediates.  However,  some complicated electron donating groups (like PNP) are difficult to be introduced and more in-depth modification methods for MOFs need to be developed.
## 6 $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol
Methanol is the critical platform in the carbon cycle, especially for the  production  of  commodity  chemicals  with  high  added  value, like dimethyl ether, acetic acid, and fatty acid methyl esters, etc. [1, 113].  Moreover,  methanol  economy  has  aroused  great attention to complete the carbon cycle. Compared to the industrial production  from  syngas,  methanol  synthesis  from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  can  be  an  environmental  and  renewable  way  to start the carbon cycle [114]. But it’s more difficult to convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for  its  thermodynamic  stability,  and  high  temperature  is  often required  to  activate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [14, 23].  The  formation  of  methanol  is thermodynamically  exothermic  (Eq.  (3))  while  RWGS  to  CO shows  the  opposite  trend  (Eq.  (1))  [115–117].  Thus,  the  lower temperature  and  higher  pressure  are  beneficial  for  improving methanol selectivity
$$
\mathbf{CO}_{2}+3\mathbf{H}_{2}\rightleftharpoons C\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{OH}+\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}~\Delta H_{\mathrm{298~K}}=49.5\mathbf{kJ}{\cdot}\mathbf{mol}^{-1}
$$
## 6.1 Cu sites
## 6.2 Zn sites
The $\mathrm{ZnZr}$ catalysts like $\mathrm{{ZnO-ZrO_{2}}}$ solid solution have also been widely studied in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol [128–130]. The $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}$ exchange experiments confirmed that hydrogen is adsorbed and  dissociated  on  Zn  sites,  while $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ TPD  showed  that  both unsaturated $\mathrm{Zr}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites  can  adsorb  and  activate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ Therefore, the strong synergism of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}$ on $Z\mathrm{n-O-Zr}$ interface is responsible for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation with the high efficiency [128].
Altogether,  the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based  MOFs  are  the  ideal  platforms  to construct Cu or $Z\mathrm{n}$ catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol. The SMSIs of metal sites and the unsaturated $\mathrm{Zr}$ nodes of MOFs can enrich the $\mathrm{{Cu}\mathrm{{-}\mathrm{{Zr}}}}$ nodes, $Z\mathrm{n-O-Zr}$ nodes, and $\mathrm{Cu-ZnO_{{x}}-Z r}$ nodes  interface  sites,  and  they  can  facilitate  the  adsorption  and activation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .  The  confinement  of  cavity  can  prevent  the agglomeration of metal sites and phase separation of the interface structures. Therefore, Cu and $Z\mathrm{n}$ sites inside Zr MOFs can show the  excellent  performances  in  terms  of  activity,  selectivity,  and stability compared with traditional catalyst $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ .
## 6.3    Noble metal sites
Pt  catalysts  generally  have  high  catalytic  activity  for  RWGS, resulting in the competition in the production between methanol and CO especially in high reaction temperature for the opposite thermodynamic properties [23, 75, 80]. High CO production will result in a drastic decrease in methanol selectivity and the poison of metal sites [134]. Therefore, it is highly required for preparing Pt  catalysts  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  methanol  under mild conditions.
By introducing photothermal sites, the localized heating of Pt sites  can  efficiently  enhance  the  methanol  yield  even  at  low temperature. For example, Zeng et al. encapsulated Pt nanocubes with average size of $6\mathrm{nm}$ and Au nanocages with average size of $31\mathrm{nm}$ together by ZIF-8 to form Au&Pt@ZIF-8. When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $3.2\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}$ $=1{:}3{\ '}$ ) in aqueous solution, Au&Pt@ZIF-8 exhibited a methanol selectivity of $96\%$ and a high methanol TOF of $^{1,522\mathrm{\h}^{-1}}$ under light irradiation, 13 times higher than that in the dark or that of Pt@ZIF  plus  Au  under  light  irradiation.  Au&Pt@ZIF-8  even showed catalytic reactivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol in $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under light irradiation, but the catalysts were kept inert until $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without light. The activation energy under or without light irradiation  was  kept  almost  the  same  around $80~\mathrm{\kJ{\cdot}m o l{\cdot}l}$ , indicating  that  light  irradiation  would  not  affect  the  catalytic mechanism.  And  Au&Pt@ZIF-8  showed  no  significant  activity decrease  during  6  cycles.  It  is  noted  that  Pt  acted  as  the  active centers  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation,  and  under  light  irradiation,  the heat generated from Au as the photothermal sites transferred to Pt.  ZIF-8  not  only  functioned  as  the  platform  for  the heterogeneous  integration  of $\mathrm{Pt}$ and  Au  to  reduce  the  spatial distance between $\mathrm{Pt}$ and Au, but also served as a “heat insulators” to prevent the dispersion of heat to solution. Thus, a localized hightemperature heating region around Pt was formed to improve the catalytic efficiency [138].
In addition to Pt catalysts, Pd catalysts have also been widely explored for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation [1, 114, 122, 123, 139], due to the resistance  to  poisoning  and  good  stability. $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ catalysts  are well known to be active for methanol production, and PdZn alloy formed under $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is regarded as the active sites while Pd is more active for RWGS [121, 140, 141]. Besides, the methanol formation rate  and  selectivity  are  strongly  influenced  by  the  size  and dispersion of the PdZn active sites. With smaller size and higher dispersion,  PdZn  sites  can  show  more  excellent  catalytic performance  toward  methanol  [141].  ZIF-8  can  be  a  good platform  for  integrating  highly  dispersed  PdZn  sites  with  small size and $\mathrm{znO}$ in a single catalyst. For example, Tsung et al. have grown an ultrathin ZIF-8 shell on $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ nanorods $(\mathrm{ZnO@2IF{-}}8)$ and then, subnanometric Pd nanoparticles were further embedded at the $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{ZIF}–8$ interface $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Pd-ZnO@ZIF-8})$ ). The uniform pores of ZIF-8 shell confined the formation of Pd nanoparticles with a small size range of $1.4\mathrm{-}1.7\mathrm{nm}$ . When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $4.5\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3\$ ), Pd-ZnO@ZIF8  exhibited $378\ \mathrm{\mmol}_{\mathrm{MeOH}^{\cdot}\mathrm{\mathbf{g}}\mathrm{pd}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ methanol  STY  and $79\%$ selectivity,  much  higher  than $213\ \mathrm{\mmol_{MeOH}{\cdot}g_{P d}{^{-1}{\cdot}h^{-1}}}$ and $62\%$ selectivity for $\mathrm{Pd}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZnO}$ with a large Pd size of $8.2\mathrm{nm}.$ , while Pd-ZIF8  showed  the  main  product  of $\mathrm{CO}$ .  Besides, $\mathrm{Pd}{\cdot}\mathrm{ZnO@}\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\$ showed the excellent stability over $25\mathrm{h}$ on steam and the average Pd size remained unchanged due to the confinement of the pores in  ZIF-8.  At  the $\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{ZIF}–8$ interface,  much  smaller  and  more dispersed PdZn sites were formed by the pore confinement after hydrogen reduction compared to PdZn over single $\mathrm{{znO}}$ . ZnO can act as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption sites and more oxygen defects in $\mathrm{znO}$ were generated during the formation of ZIF-8 shell, which can transfer electron to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and promote the activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Therefore, the ultrasmall  and  highly  dispersed  PdZn  sites  and  more  oxygen vacancy  defects  were  responsible  for  producing  methanol  with high efficiency [142].
Different  from  above,  a  tandem  process  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol has been developed, which involved the  following  three  steps: $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  formic  acid, formic  acid  reacts  with  added  alcohol  or  amine  to  get  formate ester  or  formamide,  and  further  hydrogenation  to  get  methanol [143, 144]. Such a tandem catalytic system composed of Ru based molecular  catalysts  and  Lewis  acid  sites  can  facilitate  a  positive shift in equilibrium and avoid the high energy barriers for direct hydrogenation  through  formate  pathway  [14, 143, 145, 146]. Tsung et al. have designed a tandem catalytic system based on Ru molecular  catalysts  and  MOFs,  which  was  bioinspired  from  the tandem  process  of  Calvin  cycle  on  ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase  (RuBisCO)  enzyme  (Fig. 11(a)).  The molecular catalyst $(^{\mathrm{tBu}}\mathrm{PNP})\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO}){\cdot}\mathrm{HC}$ l (catalyst 1) was encapsulated within the pores of UiO-66 (catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66)$ by the aperture-opening strategy. Then, $(^{\mathrm{tBu}}\mathrm{PNN})\mathrm{RuH(CO)Cl}$ (catalyst 3) was directly added or encapsulated within the pores of UiO-66  (catalyst $3@\mathrm{UiO}–66)$ to  participate  in  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation (Fig. 11(b)). At $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $4\:\mathrm{MPa}$ $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}=3{:}37)$ in  DMF  with  trifluoroethanol  (TFE),  catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ combined with catalyst 3 showed a high TON of $^{6,600}$ after $16\mathrm{h}$ As contrast, catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ plus catalyst $3@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ showed a lower TON value of 3,500 but exhibited the great stability and no significant loss in activity was found during successive five cycles. However, the system composed of catalyst 1, catalyst 3, and UiO66  showed  no  activity  for  methanol  production  because  of  the bimolecular catalyst deactivation. As shown in Fig. 11(c), catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ can hydrogenate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formic acid. Then, the open $Z\mathrm{r}$ sites in UiO-66 acted as the Lewis acid for catalyzing formic acid  with  adding  TFE  to  get  the  ester,  which  was  further hydrogenated to methanol by catalyst 3. And the heterogenization and isolation of molecular catalysts by the confinement of pores effectively  prevented  the  bimolecular  catalyst  deactivation  and maintained  the  activity  of  molecular  catalysts.  Such  a heterogeneous  catalytic  tandem  system  was  responsible  for producing  methanol  with  the  high  catalytic  activity  (Table  3) [147].
In  addition, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ on  the  ligand  of  UiO-66  was  further introduced into the tandem system as the Brønsted acid sites (Fig. 11(d)). When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $4\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=3{:}37.$ ) in DMF with 10 mmol TFE, catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}\ –66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ plus  catalyst  3  showed  a  higher  TON  value  of 10,900 than $6{,}900$ of the tandem system without $\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction. With the addition of $3\mathrm{\AA}$ molecular sieves to remove the formed  water,  catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}\ –66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{\ +}$ and  catalyst $3@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ showed  the  highest  reported  TON  value  of  19,000  after $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ reaction  with  adding  only $10^{-5}$ mmol  TFE.  With  the  pore confinement of MOFs, they could retain the catalytic activity after 10 cycles with a cumulative TON value of 100,000 while catalyst $1@\mathrm{UiO}\ –66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ plus catalyst 3 showed almost no activity during the second recycle. Mechanistic experiments showed that the key role of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ was to give the $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and promote the formation and desorption  of  formic  acid  for  facilitating  the  first  step  of  the tandem catalyst system catalyzed by catalyst 1 [148]. Inspired by the biological systems, each separated site played its own key role, and it connected with each other for building a tandem system.
## 7 $\cdot$ hydrogenation to $\mathbf{C}_{2+}$ products
$\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products,  such  as  light  olefins,  higher  alcohols,  and  liquid hydrocarbons, have been widely used as the precursors or solvents for  organic  synthesis  and  liquid  fuels  [10, 28, 71].  However,  it’s very  challenging  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products  due  to the  requirement  of  high  energy  input  to  form $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C}$ bond  while sometimes needing to retain the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-O}$ bond for $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ oxygenates. Besides, there are various pathways for producing $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products.
Lin  et  al.  have  synthesized  a  disk-like  MOF  composed  of $\mathrm{Zr_{12}O_{8}(\upmu_{3}-O H)_{8}(\upmu_{2}-O H)_{6}}$ (carboxylate) $^{\vert}{}_{18}$ SBUs  and  bpdc  ligands with  average  thickness  of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ .  The $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ SBUs  were  firstly deprotonated  by ${\mathrm{LiCH}}_{2}{\mathrm{SiMe}}_{3}$ and  the  alkali  metal  Li  sites  were grafted onto the $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}$ and $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}}$ sites $(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}$ -bpdc-Li). Followed by the metalation, some Li sites were replaced to form single ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites $\mathrm{\mathrm{Zr}}_{12}$ -bpdc-CuLi). As shown in Fig. 12(a), the adjacent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites on the $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ SBUs  could  form  the  bimetallic  centers, $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\right)_{2}$ sites. Meanwhile,  different  alkali  metals  like  Cs, $\mathrm{{Na}}$ ,  and  K  were introduced by an ion-exchange method to replace residual Li sites near the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites. When used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 2 MPa $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3)$ ) in tetrahydrofuran (THF), $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -bpdcCuLi showed $66.3\%$ selectivity of ethanol and a TON value of 185 after $^{10\mathrm{~\h~}}$ much  higher  than $10.1\%$ and  4  for  Cu nanoparticles $\ @{\sf Z}{\bf r}_{12}$ -bpdc-Li and $10.5\%$ and 4 for $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . After exchanging  Li  with  Cs  ( $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -bpdc-CuCs),  a  significant enhancement  activity  is  achieved  with  a  TON  value  of  490  and $>99\%$ selectivity of ethanol (Fig. $\begin{array}{r}{{12(\mathrm{b})};}\end{array}$ ). And in supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (30 MPa $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and $5\mathrm{MPa}\mathrm{H}_{2})$ and $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -bpdc-CuCs showed the  very  high  TON  value  of $^{4,080}$ with $>99\%$ selectivity  of ethanol, and the STY of ethanol was up to $6,425\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g_{C u}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . Different from Cu nanoparticles, $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\right)_{2}$ sites were responsible for the  high  yield  of  ethanol.  As  shown  in Fig. $12(\mathsf{c})$ ,  the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was activated on $(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{{I}}})_{2}$ sites by a bimetallic oxidative addition process to  form $(\mathrm{Cu^{2+}{-}H^{-}})_{2}$ and  then  reacted  with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  produce methanol  and  CHO  species.  With  the  nucleophilic  attack  on methanol  by  the  CHO  intermediate  on  bimetallic $\mathtt{C u}$ sites,  the C–C bond was formed to get ${\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{CHO}}$ intermediate. Followed by further  hydrogenation  and  bimetallic  reductive  elimination, ethanol was produced and $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\right)_{2}$ sites were regenerated. As for the alkali  metals,  DFT  calculation  and  natural  population  analysis confirmed  that  Cs  showed  a  more  effective  electron-donating effect  on ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites  than  Li.  With  the  more  electron-rich environment of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites, the bimetallic oxidative addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ could  be  facilitated  and  the  higher  hydricity  of $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{H}^{-}\right)_{2}$ was provided to improve catalytic activity. Besides, the more electronrich Cu can improve the basicity of its coordinated oxygen and promote the proton abstraction on the $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ intermediate. Thus, CHO  was  facilitated  to  form  and  stabilized  on  alkali  metals  to enhance the ethanol selectivity. Therefore, the synergy between the bimetallic $\left(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}}\right)_{2}$ sites and the electron-rich environment provided by alkali metals was responsible for the high activity and selectivity of ethanol over $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -bpdc-CuCs [27].
Based on above, the Lewis acid sites are introduced to further dehydrate the produced ethanol to get the ethylene. The multiple ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites  were  immobilized  on  the $\mathrm{Ti_{8}(\upmu_{2}-O)_{8}(\upmu_{2}-O H)_{4}}$ SBUs  of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ $(\mathrm{MIL}–125–\mathrm{NH}_{2}–\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{I}})$ by  deprotonation  using ${\mathrm{LiCH}}_{2}{\mathrm{SiMe}}_{3}$ and  metalation  on $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}$ sites.  When  used  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $5\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3)$ ) in THF, MIL$125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{-Cu}^{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyleT}}$ showed  an  ethylene  STY  of $0.514\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g_{C u}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with a selectivity of $90\%$ . And in supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (30 MPa $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ 5  MPa ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}^{\mathbf{\alpha}}$ )  and $85~^{\circ}C,$ the  STY  and  selectivity  were  further improved to $2.60\ \mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g_{C u}}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and $97\%$ , respectively. As shown in Fig. 11(c), the Ti sites of SBUs could act as the Lewis acid to form  the $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}(\upmu_{2}{\mathrm{-O-Cu^{+}}})$ sites.  Ethanol  was  produced  on  the multiple ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites  and  further  reacted  with $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O-Cu^{+}})$ to generate  the  intermediate $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}$ $\left(\upmu_{2}–\mathrm{OCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\right)$ .  Finally,  the  Lewis acid Ti sites catalyzed the $\upbeta$ -elimination to generate ethylene. It is noted that this new process is distinct from two traditional tandem pathways for hydrogenation $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to ethylene, in which one is the hydrogenation  to  methanol  followed  by  methanol  to  olefins (MTO) process, and another is the RWGS pathway followed by FT process [60].
In  the  gas–solid  reaction,  the  acidity  of  MOFs  can  also effectively  influence  the  selectivity  of  light  olefins $\mathrm{(C_{2}-C_{4}}$ olefin). $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ with  an  average  size  of $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ was  anchored  on  ZIF-8 $\mathrm{(Fe_{2}O_{3}/Z I F–8)}$ and  MIL-53(Al) $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}))$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation. At $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 3 MPa $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3)$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ showed the considerable selectivity for light olefins about $20\%$ but $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})$ exhibited  almost  no  light  olefins  production. The $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ TPD  spectra  confirmed  that  MIL-53(Al)  had  strong acidic  sites  while  ZIF-8  almost  showed  no  acidity.  The  acidity could  effectively  promote  the  hydrogenation  over  Fe  sites,  and thus the over-hydrogenation of olefins to alkanes could be avoided over $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ [154].
In  a  word,  the  SBUs  of  MOFs  can  act  as  the  platform  for fabricating  multiple ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites,  which  can  boost  the  formation  of CHO  intermediate  for  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  ethanol. However,  until  now  there  are  still  relatively  few  works  about MOFs  based  catalysts  for  the  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products, especially for the long-chain products $\mathrm{(C_{5+})}$ . More works should be done to advance the development of this field.
## 8    The stability of MOFs based catalysts for $\cdot$ hydrogenation
It’s  always  a  long-term  catalytic  process  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation especially  for  gas–solid  reaction  on  steam  under  harsh  reaction conditions like high temperature and pressure. Thus, the stability of catalysts is an important evaluation parameter for their catalytic performances and a non-negligible obstacle for further large-scale production  in  industrial  applications.  As  crystalline  network materials  linked  by  the  coordination  between  metal  nodes  and organic ligands, the factors affecting the stability of MOFs based catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  mainly  include  the  following three aspects: (1) the collapse of skeleton caused by high reaction temperature, (2) the ligands exchange by the produced methanol or water (Eq. (3)), and (3) the agglomeration and exfoliation of catalytic sites in MOFs.
The  collapse  of  skeleton  of  MOFs  based  catalysts  caused  by high  temperature  is  a  typical  case.  It  is  noted  that  the  reported MOFs  based  catalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  mainly  focus  on carboxylate MOFs. Usually, these carboxylate MOFs decompose via  a  ligand-centred  mechanism,  and  gaseous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and  volatile organic debris are released, leaving metal oxides as solid residues. This  occurs  typically  around $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for  most  MOFs,  and  it  is around $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for  MOFs  with  harder  metal  nodes  [155]. Generally,  MOF-5  analogs  typically  persist  to  temperatures around $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ MIL-101 can maintain its crystal structure until around $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and UiO-66 and analogs in its family can retain the crystallinity and pores even at the temperature range from 400 to $540~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [49, 50, 156, 157].  The  reported  MOFs  based  catalysts were  mostly  tested  lower  than  about $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation. For example, in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation, the $20\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MIL}.$ 101(DSM) maintained its catalytic activity over $200\mathrm{{h}}$ reaction on steam  at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and  1  bar $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}=1{:}4)}$ and  kept  its  crystal structure  unchanged  [67].  Besides, $\mathrm{HZSM–5@UiO–66–NH_{2}/P d}$ showed almost no decrease in the catalytic activity over $40\mathrm{~h~}$ on steam for RWGS at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $2\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3\mathrm{)}$ ) [63].
In addition to thermal stability of MOFs, the chemical stability is also the great concern. The chemical weak points mainly located at  the  metal  nodes–organic  ligands  bonds.  And  the  produced methanol  or  water  gas  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  might  cause  the ligands  exchange  by  competitively  coordinating  with  the  metal nodes  of  MOFs,  resulting  in  the  breakage  of  these  bonds.  For example, under the condition of $90\%$ relative humidity and $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ Mg-MOF-74 has a nearly complete loss of surface area after just one  day.  In  contrast,  HKUST-1  shows  the  notable  surface degradation within 3 days, and UiO-66 is stable for the full 28 days under  the  same  condition  [158].  When  exposed  with $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ vapor, the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ metal clusters in HKUST-1 can be reduced to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ , leading  to  the  formation  of  mesopores  and  macropores  [159]. However, most carboxylate MOFs can keep the structure intact in the methanol or water vapor during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation, like UiO analogs,  MIL-101  analogs,  and  MOF-808,  etc.  For  example,  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $3.2\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}$ $=1{:}3{\ '}$ ), the catalyst $\mathrm{{Cu/UiO/66(1.4)}}$ kept its catalytic activity over $50\mathrm{~h~}$ on steam and the X-ray diffraction analysis (XRD) patterns showed no difference in crystal structure before and after reaction [126].  The  MOF-808-Zn  also  maintained  the  crystal  structure when  the  methanol  and  water  vapor  are  produced  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $4\ \mathrm{MPa}$ $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.=\:1{:}3)$ ,  and  no decrease in catalytic performance was found over $100\mathrm{h}$ on stream [58]. Thus, choosing the carboxylate MOFs with good chemical stability  is  the  important  prerequisite  for  achieving $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation with good performance.
Another  concern  is  the  agglomeration  and  exfoliation  of catalytic  sites  during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation.  Compared  to  the agglomeration  and  sintering  of  catalytic  sites  on  the  traditional supported catalysts, MOFs have shown the good ability to stabilize catalytic  sites,  like  nanoparticles,  molecular  catalysts,  and  single metal  sites.  The  nanoparticles  and  molecular  catalysts  can  be confined and isolated in the pores of MOFs [61, 69, 133]. Due to the good thermal and chemical stability, the porous structure of most  carboxylate  MOFs  can  be  maintained  during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation,  thus  protecting  these  catalytic  sites  from agglomeration, exfoliation, and deactivation. In $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $4\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3\mathrm{)}$ ), both  CuZn@UiO-bpy  and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66$ displayed  almost no  decrease  in  methanol  production  rate  while $\bar{\mathrm{Cu}}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ showed $18.4\%$ loss of methanol selectivity over the $\boldsymbol{100}\mathrm{h}$ reaction. It is noted that the pore confinement effect effectively prevents the agglomeration of Cu and phase separation of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}$ interface [61, 133].  In $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  formic  acid  at $27~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 15 bar $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{:H}_{2}=1/4\right)$ in DMF, Ru molecular catalysts within the pores of UiO-66 showed no decrease in TON value of formic acid during five cycles, while homogeneous molecular catalysts could not maintain the activity even at the second cycle. The bimolecular deactivation of molecular catalysts has been avoided by the pore confinement of MOFs [69]. Besides, MOFs have the rich O sites in their SBUs, like the $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O^{-}}}$ in the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ SBUs of UiO-66 and MOF808, the $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O^{-}}$ and ${\upmu}_{3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}^{\mathrm{-}}$ in the $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ SBUs of $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}$ -bpdc MOFs, and the dangling O connected with Cr in MIL-101 [27, 58, 61]. These exposed  O  sites  after  deprotonation  can  effectively  stabilize  the single  metal  sites  as  the  catalytic  centers  for  avoiding  the agglomeration. For example, in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $3.2\mathrm{MPa}$ $\mathrm{(CO_{2}/H_{2}}=1{:}3)$ in DMF, $\mathrm{Pt}_{1}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101$ was characteristic with single Pt sites anchored on the O sites with a planar four-coordinate geometry, and showed almost no decrease in methanol selectivity over 10 cycles [59].
To sum up, until now the carboxylate MOFs are mainly applied for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation,  and  in  terms  of  thermal  and  chemical stability, most carboxylate MOFs can maintain the original crystal structures  during  the  long-term  reaction  at  temperature  lower than $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . And the catalytic sites like nanoparticles, molecular catalysts,  and  single  metal  sites  can  be  stabilized  by  the  pore confinement or strong coordination with the O sites of SBUs. But from  the  perspective  of  future  promotion  in  the  applications, more types of MOFs should be developed for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation with the long-term stability. Therefore, it is still highly required for developing  the de  novo synthesis  of  novel  MOFs,  and  the  postsynthetic structural processing and composite material engineering for achieving high catalytic efficiency [51].
## 9 Summary and opportunities
The excessive accumulation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the atmosphere has aroused serious environmental problems [2, 4–7]. From the perspective of sustainable development, selective hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to valueadded  chemicals  can  be  the  ideal  pathway  to  reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration  and  promote  the  carbon  cycle  [9–11, 160–162]. However, the thermodynamically stability and chemical inertness of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ have  brought  great  obstacles  for  the  application  by traditional supported catalysts [1, 18]. Various reaction pathways with  different  thermodynamic  properties  result  in  a  diversity  of product  distribution  and  make  the  regulation  of  selectivity difficult.
As discussed above, we have systematically reviewed the MOFs based  catalysts  for  selective  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .  The  tunable frameworks  and  well-defined  pores  encourage  the  facile construction of diverse catalytic sites within MOFs. For example, metal nanoparticles can be composited on the out surface or in the pores  of  MOFs;  the  pores  and  the  ligands  of  frameworks  can confine or graft molecular catalysts to realize their heterogenization  and  isolation;  and  the  unsaturated  metal  oxo nodes  of  MOFs  can  be  used  to  construct  the  rich  interface structures  with  Cu  or  Zn  sites,  act  as  the  Lewis  acid  sites  to synergize with catalytic center, or provide numerous $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}}$ or $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}$ to  anchor  multiple  metal  catalytic  sites.  These  diverse  catalytic structures  can  realize  the  control  of  reaction  intermediates,  and thus, enhance the catalytic activity and selectivity towards different products,  like  CO,  methane,  formic  acid,  methanol,  and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products.
Although  many  progresses  have  been  made  on  selective hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by  MOFs  based  catalysts,  there  are  still some potential problems and challenges in the current studies as follows:
(1) It is too monotonous in the design of interface structures because most of works mainly focus on Zr SBUs in respect with the diverse metal SBUs in MOFs. Moreover, the specific location and the atomically precise structure of some interface structures like $\mathrm{Cu-ZnO}_{x}–\mathrm{Zr}_{\mathrm{:}}$ ,  which  was  simply  formed  by  mixing  the $\mathrm{cu}$ and $Z\mathrm{nO}_{x}$ nanoparticles  around  the $\mathrm{Zr}$ SBUs,  are  still  unclear. These  issues  hinder  the  in-depth  research  and  application  of interface structures in MOFs.
(2)  Although  MOFs  based  catalysts  have  made  process  in improving the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation on steam, the conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the laboratory is still low $(<20\%)$ ) and unable  to  meet  the  demand  for  industrial  application  at  the relatively  low  temperature $<250^{\mathrm{~\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ and  suitable  pressure ${<}4\mathrm{MPa}.$ , especially for methanol production. The thermodynamic limitation for activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is the main obstacle, which needs to  be  overcome  for  improving  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  on  MOFs based catalysts.
(3)  The  catalytic  centers  within  MOFs  could  regulate  the reaction intermediates to some extent, but the product selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation is still severely affected by the types of metal active sites. For example, methane is always the main product over Ni sites, while it is easy to obtain CO over Pt sites. There is still great potential for the improvement of selectivity by strengthening the  regulation  of  reaction  intermediates  over  MOFs  based catalysts.
(4) Although some reaction intermediates can be observed by in situ DRIFTS and quasi in situ XPS spectra, the understanding of the structure–performance relationship and reaction mechanism for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  over  MOFs  based  catalysts  is  not  deep enough. The main obstacle is that the catalytic structures tend to change during reaction at high pressure, but the characterization of catalytic structures is still limited to ex situ measurements at low pressure,  which  might  mislead  us  to  draw  the  inaccurate structure–performance  relationships,  especially  for  the  modeling in DFT calculations.
To address above, some possible studies on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation in the future are proposed as follows:
(1) More extensive design and accurate synthesis are needed for constructing the interface structures in MOFs. More metal nodes in MOFs, like Ti, cerium (Ce), and Fe, should be applied to design the  diverse  interface  structures.  And  metal  sites  like $\mathrm{Cu}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ can be anchored on the adjacent $\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ or $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-OH}$ in metal SBUs after  deprotonation  to  form  more  interface  structures  with  the specific location and the atomically precise structure, like Zn– $(\upmu_{3^{-}}$ O)–Zr sites in MOF-808-Zn [58].
(2) In order to improve the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion at low temperature, some functional sites can be introduced within MOFs to assist the activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . For example, amino, sulfonic acid, and carboxyl groups on the ligands of MOFs can effectively adsorb and activate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}.$ . The frustrated Lewis pairs (FLPs) sites which have showed the  good  ability  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation  can  also  be  integrated  into MOFs  to  decrease  the  activation  energy  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ [163, 164]. Moreover,  MOFs  can  provide  the  platform  to  composite  with enzyme  like  formate  dehydrogenase  or  design  the  biomimetic catalytic sites for promoting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation under mild conditions [165].
(4) It is urgent to develop high pressure in situ characterization technologies,  such  as  high  pressure in  situ X-ray  absorption spectroscopy  (XAS),  XRD,  and  Raman  spectroscopy,  for characterizing  the  dynamic  structural  change  of  MOFs  based catalysts  during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  at  high  pressure  [172–174]. Therefore,  more  accurate  models  should  be  built  for  DFT calculation and the clear structure–performance relationships and accurate reaction mechanism can be further recognized.
Besides,  in  the  context  of  carbon  neutrality,  more industrialization  requirements  have  been  placed  on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  catalysts.  In  2019,  PetroChina  conducted  a  pilot plant test of methanol synthesis from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cooperated with Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion rate is higher than $20\%$ with methanol selectivity of $70\%$ in a single-pass, and the catalyst is relatively stable in long-term operation [114]. Using a ton-grade iron-zeolite bifunctional catalyst, Ge group achieved a thousand tons of gasoline-ranged hydrocarbon synthesis per year via $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  in  2020,  and  the  main  products  are aromatics  and  isoparaffins.  In  2021,  the  10,000  tons  grade aromatics test project led by a team at Tsinghua University was started with a fluidized bed reactor. The technological route starts from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ via  an  indirect  route  with  syngas  or  methanol  and finally to aromatics [10]. Although significant improvements have been  made  both  industrially  and  academically  in  the  past  few years, industrial application of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation is still restricted by  the  low  single-pass $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  rate  and  demanding reaction  conditions.  More  in-depth  studies  need  to  be  done  on MOFs  based  catalysts  for  achieving  the  future  goals  that  are improving the single pass conversion rate of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $20\%$ , and the selectivity higher than $90\%$ at the relatively mild condition. As for the specific products, the higher formation rates and catalytic efficiency  should  be  pursued.  Take  methanol  for  example,  the catalyst  productivity  with $\mathrm{TONs}>10^{6}$ and  activity  with  TOFs $\mathrm{>}10^{3}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ are highly pursued [14].
We hope that our discussion about MOFs based catalysts for selective  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can  provide  some  insights  for developing the enabled catalysts to achieve high activity, excellent selectivity,  and  good  stability.  We  believe  that  MOFs  based catalysts  have  the  great  development  prospects  and  application potential  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  under  mild  conditions  in  the future.
